jackie robinson's story and breaking the  color barrier in baseball is known  around the world  but to fully understand the black  movement in hockey the story begins with  herb carnegie he should have been in the  national hockey league before me he had  all the skills and all the talent you  know he had went to  two training camps i believe with  toronto and the new york rangers you  know was one of the better players there  but unfortunately he just they just  didn't chose him but he had all the  skills and talent you know to to be an  nhler i learned a lot from her talking  to him and working in some of his  programs  in the 1940s  carnegie was a three-time mvp in the  quebec league but blatant racism kept  him out of the nhl unfortunately  carnegie never played in the all-white  nhl but in 1958 one man had the mental  fortitude and the skill to break the nhl  colored barrier when william reese  stepped onto the ice at the montreal  forum playing for the boston bruins  history was made i had butterflies i was  a bit nervous but then the first shift  and i just settled down and played my  game and it was a great feeling we beat  him three nothing and uh what a feeling  it was i really didn't realize that i'd  broke the color barrier or open the  doors for not only  other black players and players of color  until i read it in the paper the next  day but it wasn't until 16 years later  that mike marston was drafted by the  washington capitals  but progress has been slow  as evidenced by recent racially  motivated events the nhl has made a  concerted effort to bring more diversity  into the game and a major step was  appointing william their ambassador of  diversity in 1996. it's a nice feeling  to know that i've made a  difference in a lot of boys and girls  lives over the years that i've been with  the nhl just telling them to set goals  for yourselves  believe in yourself and don't let  anybody tell you you can't attain your  goal if you set your mind on doing  something then go for it
 

